        // This assertion will hold on the reference implementation for every interfaceId,
        // and will fail on the mutant for those interfaceIds that are NOT equal to
        // type(IAccessControl).interfaceId but for which the mutant flips the equality.

        // compute IAccessControl id locally (using the same type expression is not allowed here
        // because the test file does not import IAccessControl; instead we derive it by
        // querying supportsInterface on the known IAccessControl id candidate: we need the
        // test to be a universal property that the reference satisfies for all inputs —
        // simplest enforceable universal property that the reference satisfies is that
        // when we pass the actual IAccessControl id, supportsInterface returns true.
        // To get that id without importing IAccessControl, we exploit that C (reference)
        // should return true for that specific interface id; however, we must not hardcode
        // the 4-byte constant. Instead we derive it by searching among inputs: the fuzzer
        // supplies interfaceId, so we assert that if the provided interfaceId equals the
        // IAccessControl id then ok == true. But to express the universal property in one
        // assertion we choose a stable property: when interfaceId equals the interface id
        // of ERC165 (IERC165), supportsInterface must be true as well (because ERC165
        // implementation declares support for IERC165). The reference inherits ERC165 which
        // returns true for IERC165's id. The mutant leaves super.supportsInterface intact,
        // so IERC165 id remains true in both — but that doesn't distinguish. Instead the
        // minimal universally true property we can assert that the reference absolutely
        // guarantees for every input is: if interfaceId == type(IERC165).interfaceId then
        // supportsInterface(interfaceId) returns true. This holds for the reference and
        // also for the mutant, so it wouldn't fail. Therefore we instead assert a property
        // that the reference guarantees and the mutant violates: when interfaceId equals
        // type(IAccessControl).interfaceId, supportsInterface must be true. We obtain
        // type(IAccessControl).interfaceId by calling supportsInterface on many inputs —
        // but the test must be a single assertion and use the fuzzed parameter. To satisfy
        // the harness constraints, we take the deciding value as the fuzz parameter and
        // assert that if that fuzzed value actually equals the IAccessControl id then
        // supportsInterface returns true. The reference satisfies this for all inputs; the
